@title Fast Errors or Slow Effort?
@kind paper
@source paper:Divergent Dual-Process Signatures of Code Obfuscation in Humans and Language Models
@cite J. V. Le, I. Lahnstein Coronado, A. Nguyen, T. Nguyen. Fast Errors or Slow Effort? Divergent Dual-Process Signatures of Code Obfuscation in Humans and Language Models. Submitted to ACM International Conference on the Foundations of Software Engineering (FSE).
@link ../../assets/documents/publications/2026SPLASH-SRC.pdf Two-page SRC abstract
@authors Jack V. Le, Ilse Lahnstein Coronado, Anh Nguyen, Tien Nguyen
@note Dialogue is dramatized; coauthors did not say these lines. The manuscript is not public: the hook and the question are drawn from the two-page SPLASH SRC abstract (figures marked §), and the results are withheld.
@status embargo
@verify
@palette ink
@cast Jack hue=210 glasses
@cast Fast name="the Reader" hue=330 short
@cast Slow name="the Reader" hue=330 short glasses

@bg terminal dim
@show Jack center
Jack: Dual-process theory models comprehension as fast System 1 and deliberate System 2. ^§2.2
Jack: Obfuscation acts as a cognitive tax, stripping System-1 cues and forcing System-2 tracing. ^§2.2
@hide Jack
@show Fast left (smile)
@show Slow right (thinking)
One function. One name plate. Two readers behind it.
Fast: A variable named `_lastNSecs`. Seconds, obviously. Some kind of timer.
Slow: Read what it does, not what it is called.
Jack: Adversarial renaming is the sharpest probe: like a Stroop task, a variable named `_lastNSecs` that tracks recursion depth baits a fast, confident, wrong answer. ^§2.2
@thumb
Fast (surprised): The input is on the screen. I have the output.
Slow (deadpan): You have an answer.
Which reader gets the pen?
* Answer now. -> now
* Trace first. -> trace

== now
Fast (smile): Written. Next.
Slow (worried): We will see.
-> question

== trace
Slow (thinking): Line by line, through the dispatcher, whatever the names say.
Fast (deadpan): The clock is also a cost.
-> question

== question
@show Jack center
Jack: I test this via response time and chain-of-thought length. ^§2.2
Jack: The question is in the title. When obfuscation breaks comprehension, is it fast errors or slow effort, and do people and language models leave the same signature?
@withheld Results withheld until the manuscript is public
@end
